side by side holding hands  a show of solidarity in munich  a human chain across the city protesting  against russia's invasion of ukraine  nearby the russian consulate its  entrance vandalized with red paint  a show of anger against vladimir putin  the man who protesters say must face  justice for his actions  i despise war any kind of war and i  really want to set an example i think  putin has been courted for far too long  for 20 years the west has been trying to  keep him quiet so that he doesn't get  upset and that simply has to stop now  in rome the mood may have been more  festive but the message to putin just as  critical  italy may have been one of russia's  closest allies in europe with many  politicians having previously boasted of  strong ties but now many are seeking to  distance themselves  across europe cities that have welcomed  russian money are having to reflect on  the effect that it's had on their  businesses and politics  among them london a major beneficiary of  russian cash since the end of the soviet  union but that has not calmed any  criticism of putin or his allies here  the more missiles raining down on  ukraine the more solidarity people here  are showing towards ukrainians and what  people here want is for the west to step  up and take decisive action against  putin  and his assault on ukraine there's going  to be a genocide why do we wait they  will go in  but only after there's a genocide how  outrageous  we need to do it now  in the hague home of the international  criminal court many called for an end to  the invasion and blamed putin himself  not russians for the conflict  all the while ukraine continues to come  under attack  yet many europeans are determined to  signal their support for the country and  to show ukrainians they are not alone  sonia gallego al jazeera london
 

